{"causes": ["The flourishing Franco-Flemish school of polyphony provided a rich musical tradition for Coclico to build upon.", "The Protestant Reformation's emphasis on vernacular liturgy and congregational singing spurred innovation in sacred music.", "The intellectual climate of the Renaissance encouraged theoretical treatises and the articulation of new artistic principles."], "effects": ["Coclico's *Compendium musices descriptum* contributed to the theoretical understanding and practice of "musica reservata."", "His motets influenced the development of expressive vocal music in the mid-16th century.", "Coclico's career demonstrated the challenges faced by musicians navigating shifting religious and political allegiances during the Reformation."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "josquin-des-prez", "sourceName": "Josquin des Prez", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "adrianus-petit-coclico", "targetName": "Adrianus Petit Coclico", "context": "Adrianus Petit Coclico claimed to have been a pupil of the esteemed composer Josquin des Prez during the early 16th century, though this claim is debated by historians."}, {"sourceSlug": "adrianus-petit-coclico", "sourceName": "Adrianus Petit Coclico", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "nuremberg-germany", "targetName": "Nuremberg, Germany", "context": "Adrianus Petit Coclico published his seminal treatise, *Compendium musices descriptum*, in Nuremberg in 1552, a significant center for printing and Reformation thought."}, {"sourceSlug": "adrianus-petit-coclico", "sourceName": "Adrianus Petit Coclico", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "compendium-musices-descriptum", "targetName": "Compendium musices descriptum", "context": "In 1552, Adrianus Petit Coclico authored *Compendium musices descriptum*, a pivotal music theory treatise defining "musica reservata"."}, {"sourceSlug": "adrianus-petit-coclico", "sourceName": "Adrianus Petit Coclico", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "musica-reservata", "targetName": "Musica Reservata", "context": "Adrianus Petit Coclico was a key figure in defining and advocating for "musica reservata" through his theoretical writings and compositions in the mid-16th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "martin-luther", "sourceName": "Martin Luther", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "adrianus-petit-coclico", "targetName": "Adrianus Petit Coclico", "context": "The musical reforms and theological ideas of Martin Luther significantly influenced Adrianus Petit Coclico's career and his engagement with Protestant musical patronage."}], "places": [{"name": "Copenhagen, Denmark", "role": "Place of Death"}, {"name": "Nuremberg, Germany", "role": "Publication City"}, {"name": "Königsberg, Prussia", "role": "Service Location"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Adrianus Petit Coclico", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "adrianus-petit-coclico"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q376156", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adrianus_Petit_Coclico"], "tags": ["Heads of State & Government", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Adrianus Petit Coclico holds significant regional and thematic importance for his role in articulating the principles of "musica reservata" in his 1552 treatise, which influenced the development of expressive vocal music in the mid-16th century. His career also provides insight into the challenges faced by musicians during the Reformation.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-15T07:15:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31871381344", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Adrianus Petit Coclico was a Netherlandish composer of the Renaissance.", "newValue": "Adrianus Petit Coclico (c. 1500 – 1 September 1562) was a Netherlandish composer, singer, and music theorist, active during the high Renaissance. Though his birthdate is unknown, his death in Copenhagen in 1562 marks the end of a turbulent career that spanned several European courts. Coclico is significant for his contributions to music theory, particularly his role in articulating the aesthetics of "musica reservata," and for his direct, albeit controversial, association with the musical developments of the early Protestant Reformation.

Coclico's most enduring legacy is his 1552 treatise, *Compendium musices descriptum*, published in Nuremberg. In this work, he became one of the first theorists to explicitly define and advocate for "musica reservata," a style emphasizing a close, expressive relationship between text and music, often through chromaticism and rhetorical devices. He claimed to have studied with the revered master Josquin des Prez, a claim that, while debated, underscores his aspiration to high artistic lineage. His career saw him serve various patrons, including Duke Albrecht of Prussia in Königsberg (1545-1547) and later Emperor Ferdinand I, before his final years in Copenhagen.

Despite a relatively small body of surviving work, including his 1552 collection of motets, *Consolationes piae ex psalmis Davidicis*, Coclico's theoretical writings remain a crucial primary source for understanding mid-16th century compositional practices and the evolving aesthetic of musical expression. His life, marked by frequent changes of patronage, accusations of misconduct, and shifts between Catholic and Protestant circles, reflects the broader religious and political instability of the Reformation era, making his influence on subsequent generations complex but notable."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-15T07:15:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31871381344", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1499, Flanders"}, {"title": "Democratic movements and constitutional governance", "type": "Idea", "year": "1499, Flanders"}], "newValue": ["The flourishing Franco-Flemish school of polyphony provided a rich musical tradition for Coclico to build upon.", "The Protestant Reformation's emphasis on vernacular liturgy and congregational singing spurred innovation in sacred music.", "The intellectual climate of the Renaissance encouraged theoretical treatises and the articulation of new artistic principles."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-15T07:15:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31871381344", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Influenced national policy and international relations", "type": "Idea", "year": "1562-09-01"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1562-09-01"}], "newValue": ["Coclico's *Compendium musices descriptum* contributed to the theoretical understanding and practice of "musica reservata."", "His motets influenced the development of expressive vocal music in the mid-16th century."